  "expected_transcript": "Let your hands rest gently on your knees or lap, palms open. Notice the weight of your body supported by the chair’s edges beneath you. Feel the slight pressure where your feet touch the floor. If your back feels long, let your shoulders drift softly away from your ears. The chair holds you without asking you to hold it. For now, let your posture find stillness like a branch settling into its natural shape.\n\nLet your attention rest lightly on the air moving in and out through your nose. Now, shift your focus to what you hear around you—just as it is. Notice the faint hum of ventilation, a distant conversation, or the creak of wood outside. Choose one sound that feels present enough to hold your attention briefly. When you notice your mind wandering, simply return to that sound without labeling it good or bad. Let it anchor you again. For now, focus only on hearing—not interpreting or naming the sounds. Trust that this gentle return keeps your awareness steady.\n\nLet your fingertips press softly against your thighs, grounding you where you sit. If it feels natural, let your shoulders settle just a fraction lower. No rush, no need to move further. Simply notice you’re here, now, after the day’s activity.\n",
